### PRIORITY 1: Proactive Anticipatory Care Planning for High-Risk Patients

**Consolidated from:** Personalised care plans, anticipatory prescribing, care coordination for multimorbidity

**Description:** Systematic identification and pre-emptive management of patients at high risk of winter deterioration, including proactive outreach, agreed care plans, and advance prescribing, before crisis occurs.

| Criterion | Score | Rationale |
|---|---|---|
| Magnitude | 5 | High-risk patients (frail elderly, multimorbidity, COPD, heart failure) drive disproportionate A&E admissions in winter. NHS England data consistently shows top 5% of patients account for >50% of unplanned admissions (NHS England, 2023) |
| Evidence strength | 4 | Strong RCT and cohort evidence supports anticipatory care models in reducing emergency admissions. The PRISM risk stratification tool and Year of Care programmes have UK-specific evidence (Lewis et al., 2021) |
| Cost-effectiveness | 4 | Preventing one emergency admission saves approximately £2,500–£3,500 (NHS Reference Costs). Modelling suggests anticipatory care is cost-saving at scale (Kasteridis et al., 2015) |
| NHS/Policy alignment | 5 | Central to NHS Long Term Plan (2019) personalised care agenda; aligned with Elective Recovery and Primary Care Recovery plans |
| Short-term impact | 3 | Requires register building, training, and relationship development; some impact within 1–3 years but not immediate |
| Medium-term impact | 5 | Well-embedded programmes demonstrably reduce winter admissions at 3–5 year mark |
| Long-term impact | 5 | Systemic cultural shift toward proactive care; transformative over 10 years |
| Health inequalities | 4 | Targets high-need populations but requires active outreach to reach most deprived; risk of inverse care law if purely opt-in |

**Weighted Score Calculation:**
(5×15) + (4×15) + (4×10) + (5×10) + (3×10) + (5×10) + (5×10) + (4×20) = 75 + 60 + 40 + 50 + 30 + 50 + 50 + 80 = **435/500 = 87/100**

