### PRIORITY 2: Integrated Virtual Wards and Hospital-at-Home

**Consolidated from:** Virtual wards, hospital-at-home, remote monitoring for acute patients

**Description:** Scaling NHS virtual ward capacity to allow acutely unwell patients to receive hospital-level monitoring and care in their homes, reducing physical bed pressure and infection risk.

| Criterion | Score | Rationale |
|---|---|---|
| Magnitude | 5 | Bed occupancy consistently exceeds 95% in winter, beyond safe operational thresholds of 85% (Royal College of Emergency Medicine, 2022) |
| Evidence strength | 4 | Growing RCT evidence including Cochrane reviews supports hospital-at-home for selected conditions (Caplan et al., 2012, updated literature); NHS England virtual ward programme has early evaluation data |
| Cost-effectiveness | 4 | NHS England estimated virtual wards cost approximately 25–30% less per patient than inpatient care for appropriate cohorts (NHS England, 2022) |
| NHS/Policy alignment | 5 | Core NHS winter resilience strategy; 10,000 virtual ward beds target set by NHS England (2022) |
| Short-term impact | 4 | Rapid deployment possible where infrastructure exists; meaningful bed-freeing within 1–3 years |
| Medium-term impact | 5 | Structural shift in acute care model if scaled nationally |
| Long-term impact | 4 | Dependent on digital infrastructure maturity and workforce adaptation |
| Health inequalities | 3 | Risk of excluding digitally disadvantaged, elderly, and those without suitable home environments; equity safeguards essential |

